A||||7||MrPBody||My motherboard expired||||1||
Z||000000||MrPBody||05-06-2001||11:12 AM||||After 6 years and many upgrades, my old HP's motherboard has passed on to Circuit Board Heaven.  May it rust in peace.<P>I'm back with a new system, but I need to figure out how to transfer the contents of my old drive to my new one.  I have <I>lots</I> of photos on the old drive.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||05-06-2001||11:55 AM||||Can't you just hook up your old drive to the new computer?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||05-06-2001||12:46 PM||||Yes, you should be able to just plug in the old drive. Most systems have one IDE slot left over. If not, you can unplug a CD drive long enough to do it. When you restart, the drive should show up automatically.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||05-06-2001||01:58 PM||||Make sure you first deactivate it from being the primary "boot" partition and/or set it as a slave drive if you go with a new computer with a larger harddrive.  Then you can access your old drive with all the original folders and at the same time it will not boot with the operating system which may look at your new computer components and conflict with what it recognized from your old system's components.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||05-06-2001||02:05 PM||||I was going to install my old drive as a secondary (if that's the right word) drive, but I think there may be provision for mounting only one drive.  I haven't taken the cover off, yet.<P>The old drive uses FAT16.  Can it coexist with a FAT32 primary drive?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||05-06-2001||04:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>I was going to install my old drive as a secondary (if that's the right word) drive, but I think there may be provision for mounting only one drive.  I haven't taken the cover off, yet.<P>The old drive uses FAT16.  Can it coexist with a FAT32 primary drive?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can probably install it as a secondary (slaved) drive. There SHOULD be a vacant plug on the cable that goes to your new hard drive. All you need is that, and a power connector. Be <I>sure</I> to set the jumper on the old drive to slave.  <BR> <BR>Your new system will be able to recognize FAT16. <P>Once you have it connected, you <I>might</I> need to go into BIOS and switch "IDE-0, secondary" to autodetect. I have seen cases where Windows (especially NT) would find it, even if BIOS wasn't looking at it. <P>If your new machine is UDMA 66 or 100, I would probably just use the secondary IDE channel (IDE-1), and leave the old drive configured as a master. You might have to unplug the CDROM or whatever. It should STILL boot from your new drive. <BR>If the HDD data cable has very fine wires, but twice as many, it's probably UDMA. <BR>You will be able to see the difference when you look at them.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-06-2001||04:38 PM||||Copying the contents from one drive to another is fine if you start with a fresh install of Windows on the new drive, and don't mind re-installing every single program  again. If you really want something like a carbon copy of your old drive on your new, check the new drive-vendor's website. They often have free utilities for download that will do just that.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||05-06-2001||11:16 PM||||And people think computer's are simple! heheh<BR>Actually, they are after you learn the basics... Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
